 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Yes.** Investors pay about 0.58 percentage points more than owner-occupiers on a standard variable home loan, and the gap has held at that level since at least January 2023.\n\nAs of September 2026 (the latest RBA data):\n\n| Loan type (advertised rate) | Owner-occupier | Investor | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Variable, standard | 8.77% | 9.35% | +0.58 pts |\n| Variable, discounted | 6.80% | 7.13% | +0.33 pts |\n\nThe standard-rate gap has been 0.58 points in every month I checked, so both groups have moved in step with the cash rate. The discounted gap is narrower than it was in early 2023 (about 0.51 points in January 2023), so the premium on discounted loans has shrunk.\n\nThese are advertised rates from RBA table F5. Rates actually paid on new loans are typically lower, especially after discounting. I haven't checked the actual-rate table (F6), so I can't say whether the investor premium is the same there.",
